    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "YcjN is a putative substrate binding protein found in bacteria. Within the Escherichia coli genome, YcjN is expressed from a cluster of genes involved in carbohydrate import and metabolism. Although several proteins from this operon have been characterized in vitro, YcjN remains poorly characterized and its structure has not been reported. In this study, the molecular structure and post-translational modifications of recombinant E. coli YcjN were explored using X-ray crystallography and intact mass spectrometry assays. Specifically, the first crystal structure of YcjN was reported to a resolution of 1.95  Angstrom. A structural comparison to other substrate binding proteins revealed that its three-dimensional structure is similar to other SBPs, including the well-characterized maltose binding protein (MBP). Additionally, analysis using intact mass spectrometry of recombinantly produced YcjN revealed that it is lipidated at cysteine 21. Furthermore, comparisons of size-exclusion chromatography profiles of lipidated and non-lipidated YcjN proteins revealed that lipidated YcjN aggregates in solution via its lipid moiety. Collectively, these findings provide a better understanding of the structural and functional properties of recombinantly overexpressed bacterial lipoproteins and establish a foundation for future studies aimed at elucidating the role of substrate binding proteins in bacterial physiology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Treviño, M. and Sharaf, N. (2024). Expression, purification, and characterization of diacylated Lipo-YcjN from Escherichia coli. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/kd722dz9751. https://doi.org/10.25740/kd722dz9751.",
